first-line-heading (MD041), Headings
On by default, severity warning. Not fixable.
Your configuration turns it off.
first-line-h1 is one of markdownlint's names for this rule.

Comments aside, the file starts with a top-level heading or front matter title.

Why: The top-level heading is usually the document's title.

Tags: headings

Options, under rules.first-line-heading.options:
  level (integer, default 1)
    Level that the first heading must have.
  front_matter_title (string|false, default ^\s*"?title"?\s*[:=])
    Regular expression, matched in any case, for the front matter line that
    gives the document a title, which counts as the first heading. An empty
    string or false turns this off.
  allow_preamble (boolean, default false)
    Allow content before the first heading.

Fix: There is no automatic fix. Only the author can write the title.

This document breaks the rule:
  | This guide explains how to install the tool.
  |
  | # Guide

gomdlint reports:
  1:1 First line should be a top-level heading

This document follows the rule:
  | # Guide
  |
  | This guide explains how to install the tool.

Configure:
  rules:
    first-line-heading:
      options:
        level: 1

Under rules, first-line-heading: false turns the rule off and
first-line-heading: error reports its findings as errors. The flag
--disable first-line-heading turns it off for one run.

Silence one line:
  <!-- markdownlint-disable-next-line first-line-heading -->

markdownlint: https://github.com/DavidAnson/markdownlint/blob/v0.41.1/doc/md041.md
